---
import Tool from '@/layouts/Tool.astro';
---

<Tool slug="video-file-size-calculator">
  <div data-size-tool>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2" role="radiogroup" aria-label="What to work out">
      <button type="button" class="chip" role="radio" aria-checked="true" data-mode="size">
        File size from bitrate
      </button>
      <button type="button" class="chip" role="radio" aria-checked="false" data-mode="bitrate">
        Bitrate for a size limit
      </button>
    </div>

    <fieldset class="mt-8">
      <legend class="field-label">Length of the video</legend>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-3">
        <label>
          <span class="sr-only">Hours</span>
          <input class="input" type="number" min="0" step="1" value="0" data-h />
          <span class="mt-1.5 block text-[13px] text-fg-subtle">hours</span>
        </label>
        <label>
          <span class="sr-only">Minutes</span>
          <input class="input" type="number" min="0" step="1" value="10" data-m />
          <span class="mt-1.5 block text-[13px] text-fg-subtle">minutes</span>
        </label>
        <label>
          <span class="sr-only">Seconds</span>
          <input class="input" type="number" min="0" step="1" value="0" data-s />
          <span class="mt-1.5 block text-[13px] text-fg-subtle">seconds</span>
        </label>
      </div>
    </fieldset>

    <div class="mt-6 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <label data-show="size">
        <span class="field-label">Video bitrate (Mbps)</span>
        <input class="input" type="number" min="0" step="0.1" value="8" data-vbr />
      </label>
      <label data-show="bitrate" hidden>
        <span class="field-label">Size limit (MB)</span>
        <input class="input" type="number" min="1" step="1" value="500" data-limit />
      </label>
      <label>
        <span class="field-label">Audio bitrate (kbps)</span>
        <select class="input" data-abr>
          <option value="0">No audio</option>
          <option value="96">96 kbps</option>
          <option value="128">128 kbps</option>
          <option value="192" selected>
            192 kbps
          </option>
          <option value="256">256 kbps</option>
          <option value="320">320 kbps</option>
        </select>
      </label>
    </div>

    <div class="readout mt-8" aria-live="polite">
      <p class="text-[14px] text-fg-muted" data-out-label>
        Estimated file size
      </p>
      <p class="text-stat mt-2" data-out>
        -
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-[14px] text-fg-subtle" data-out-note></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <Fragment slot="about">
    <h2>How video file size works</h2>
    <p>
      A video's size is its bitrate multiplied by its length. Bitrate is how many bits each second
      of the file takes, usually given in megabits per second (Mbps) for the picture and kilobits
      per second (kbps) for the sound. Add the two, multiply by the number of seconds, divide by
      eight to turn bits into bytes, and you have the size.
    </p>
    <p>
      An 8 Mbps picture with 192 kbps sound runs to about 61 MB a minute, so ten minutes is about
      614 MB. Double the bitrate and the file doubles. The resolution and the frame rate do not
      enter the sum on their own: they change how much bitrate a clean picture needs.
    </p>

    <h2>Fitting a video under a size limit</h2>
    <p>
      Switch to <strong>Bitrate for a size limit</strong>, enter the length and the limit, and the
      calculator gives the highest video bitrate that fits. It keeps 2 percent back for the
      container's own data (the index and the headers an MP4 carries), so the export lands under the
      limit rather than on it. If the answer is very low for the resolution, the picture will turn
      blocky: export at a smaller size, cut the video shorter, or use a newer codec.
    </p>

    <h2>Rough starting points</h2>
    <p>
      YouTube publishes the bitrates it recommends for uploads, and they make sensible defaults for
      H.264 in general. For standard (SDR) video:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Resolution</th>
          <th>24 to 30 fps</th>
          <th>48 to 60 fps</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>720p</td>
          <td>5 Mbps</td>
          <td>7.5 Mbps</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>1080p</td>
          <td>8 Mbps</td>
          <td>12 Mbps</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>1440p</td>
          <td>16 Mbps</td>
          <td>24 Mbps</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>4K (2160p)</td>
          <td>35 to 45 Mbps</td>
          <td>53 to 68 Mbps</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      HEVC and AV1 reach the same look at noticeably lower bitrates than H.264, which matters when
      you are squeezing under a limit. The trade-off is that older phones and some websites play
      H.264 only. <a href="/guides/h264-hevc-or-av1">H.264, HEVC or AV1</a> walks through which to
      pick.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the real file can differ</h2>
    <p>
      Most exports use a variable bitrate: quiet, still shots take fewer bits and busy ones take
      more, around the average you set. The final size usually lands within a few percent of the
      estimate, but a very still video can come out smaller. Sizes here are decimal megabytes (1 MB
      is 1,000,000 bytes), the unit macOS and most websites use; Windows counts in units of
      1,048,576 bytes, so it shows the same file about 5 percent smaller.
    </p>
    <p>
      In Concat the Export sheet starts with a recommended bitrate for the timeline's size and frame
      rate, and you can type your own. The <a href="/guides/export">export guide</a> covers every
      option on it.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</Tool>

<script>
  function setup() {
    const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-size-tool]');
    if (!root || root.dataset.ready) return;
    root.dataset.ready = '';
    const q = <T extends Element>(sel: string) => root.querySelector<T>(sel)!;
    const num = (sel: string) => Math.max(0, Number(q<HTMLInputElement>(sel).value) || 0);
    let mode: 'size' | 'bitrate' = 'size';

    const formatSize = (bytes: number) =>
      bytes >= 1e9
        ? `${(bytes / 1e9).toFixed(2)} GB`
        : `${(bytes / 1e6).toFixed(bytes >= 1e8 ? 0 : 1)} MB`;

    function render() {
      const seconds = num('[data-h]') * 3600 + num('[data-m]') * 60 + num('[data-s]');
      const audioKbps = Number(q<HTMLSelectElement>('[data-abr]').value);
      const out = q('[data-out]');
      const note = q('[data-out-note]');
      q('[data-out-label]').textContent =
        mode === 'size' ? 'Estimated file size' : 'Highest video bitrate that fits';
      if (seconds <= 0) {
        out.textContent = '-';
        note.textContent = 'Enter how long the video is.';
        return;
      }
      if (mode === 'size') {
        const videoKbps = num('[data-vbr]') * 1000;
        const bytes = ((videoKbps + audioKbps) * 1000 * seconds) / 8;
        out.textContent = formatSize(bytes);
        note.textContent = `About ${formatSize(bytes / (seconds / 60))} a minute, at ${(
          (videoKbps + audioKbps) /
          1000
        ).toFixed(2)} Mbps in total.`;
      } else {
        const limitBytes = num('[data-limit]') * 1e6 * 0.98;
        const totalKbps = (limitBytes * 8) / seconds / 1000;
        const videoKbps = totalKbps - audioKbps;
        if (videoKbps <= 0) {
          out.textContent = 'Too small';
          note.textContent = 'The sound alone fills that limit. Pick a lower audio bitrate.';
          return;
        }
        out.textContent =
          videoKbps >= 1000
            ? `${(videoKbps / 1000).toFixed(2)} Mbps`
            : `${Math.floor(videoKbps)} kbps`;
        note.textContent = `${Math.floor(videoKbps).toLocaleString('en')} kbps for the picture plus ${audioKbps} kbps for the sound, with 2% kept back for the container.`;
      }
    }

    for (const button of root.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-mode]')) {
      button.addEventListener('click', () => {
        mode = button.dataset.mode as 'size' | 'bitrate';
        for (const b of root.querySelectorAll('[data-mode]'))
          b.setAttribute('aria-checked', String(b === button));
        for (const el of root.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-show]'))
          el.hidden = el.dataset.show !== mode;
        render();
      });
    }
    root.addEventListener('input', render);
    render();
  }
  // Now, since a page's module script can run after the first astro:page-load, and after every
  // client-side navigation. setup() skips a tool it has already wired.
  setup();
  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', setup);
</script>
